  BASELINE (p_gpi2_assumed=0): err_aria=5.254  err_forte=5.973  worst-case=5.973  spread=0.719
  BEST WORST-CASE candidate:   p_gpi2_assumed=0.0  worst-case=5.973  (vs baseline 5.973)
  BEST SPREAD (most consistent across backends): p_gpi2_assumed=0.0008  spread=0.445  (vs baseline 0.719)

  -- HONEST READ --
    No nonzero GPi2 correction improves the worst-case error across BOTH real backends vs the zero-correction baseline -- a real, honest negative result: any single assumed p_gpi2 that helps one backend's real data does not reliably help the other's, which is itself a direct, real demonstration of exactly the calibration-fragility problem this whole investigation is about.
    Separately: p_gpi2_assumed=0.0008 gives the most CONSISTENT (least backend-dependent) correction across the two real backends, even if not necessarily the lowest worst-case error.
